Learning goals
By the end of this unit, students will be able to:

explain why tidy storage matters for safety;
organise tools, pots, and materials in a practical way;
maintain a safe and accessible workspace;
complete a basic equipment check;
record issues or missing items clearly.

Learning goals
By the end of this unit, students will be able to:

describe why restoration work matters;
identify common invasive weeds such as gorse;
care for young trees and tree guards;
record changes in plant health over time;
explain how restoration supports biodiversity.

Learning goals
By the end of this unit, students will be able to:

explain why regular feeding and watering matter;
carry out safe and calm animal handling;
observe signs of health, comfort, or concern;
record simple welfare observations clearly;
report issues to the farm supervisor appropriately.

Learning goals
By the end of this unit, students will be able to:

explain why kōura are culturally and ecologically important;
describe key anatomy and why orbit-carapace length is used as a measurement;
identify how shade, water temperature, and habitat structure affect kōura;
plan and carry out a safe stream investigation;
collect and present simple data using tables, graphs, and written explanations;
suggest practical actions that support stream health and kaitiakitanga.

Learning goals
By the end of this unit, students will be able to:

explain why watering, weeding, and mulching matter;
identify healthy versus stressed plants;
care for seedlings safely and gently;
use basic horticulture tools responsibly;
record garden observations clearly.

Learning goals
By the end of this unit, students will be able to:

observe stream conditions and describe visible signs of health;
notice different wildlife and plant changes across seasons;
collect basic data about bee activity safely;
compare observations over time;
explain why monitoring matters for land and water care.
